{"title":"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 8","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems\">2000 AMC 12 #8</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_10_Problems\" title=\"2000 AMC 10 Problems\">2000 AMC 10 #12</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Video:\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video:</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(Newton&#39;s_Forward_Differences)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (Newton's Forward Differences)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solution_7_(Newton’s_little_formula)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7 (Newton’s little formula)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Solution_8_(geometrical_intuition)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 8 (geometrical intuition)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Solution_9\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 9</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Power Solve</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">14</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Figures <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> consist of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/0/030e19e4e2f1c074127cb50581e8496470c5419f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/6/66644fd9f411299c3c1eabf151b5f601c9ef6c21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> nonoverlapping unit squares, respectively. If the pattern were continued, how many nonoverlapping unit squares would there be in figure 100?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5d019fdb6e828e11ee09df712151a79c67ab745.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(8); draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle); draw((9,0)--(10,0)--(10,3)--(9,3)--cycle); draw((8,1)--(11,1)--(11,2)--(8,2)--cycle); draw((19,0)--(20,0)--(20,5)--(19,5)--cycle); draw((18,1)--(21,1)--(21,4)--(18,4)--cycle); draw((17,2)--(22,2)--(22,3)--(17,3)--cycle); draw((32,0)--(33,0)--(33,7)--(32,7)--cycle); draw((29,3)--(36,3)--(36,4)--(29,4)--cycle); draw((30,2)--(35,2)--(35,5)--(30,5)--cycle); draw((31,1)--(31,6)--(34,6)--(34,1)--cycle); label(&quot;Figure&quot;,(0.5,-1),S); label(&quot;$0$&quot;,(0.5,-2.5),S); label(&quot;Figure&quot;,(9.5,-1),S); label(&quot;$1$&quot;,(9.5,-2.5),S); label(&quot;Figure&quot;,(19.5,-1),S); label(&quot;$2$&quot;,(19.5,-2.5),S); label(&quot;Figure&quot;,(32.5,-1),S); label(&quot;$3$&quot;,(32.5,-2.5),S); [/asy]\" width=\"505\" height=\"152\" />\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/7/3c7d5888e6e242c860764177eaabdae2185b38b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 10401 \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 19801 \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 20201 \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 39801 \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 40801$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"529\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video:\">Video:</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Video:\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HVP6qjKAkjA&amp;t=2s\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HVP6qjKAkjA&amp;t=2s</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/813df98e4f773a3586f669185811921710cc5d1a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] unitsize(8); fill((29,3)--(30,3)--(30,4)--(29,4)--cycle); fill((31,3)--(32,3)--(32,4)--(31,4)--cycle); fill((33,3)--(34,3)--(34,4)--(33,4)--cycle); fill((35,3)--(36,3)--(36,4)--(35,4)--cycle); fill((30,2)--(31,2)--(31,3)--(30,3)--cycle); fill((32,2)--(33,2)--(33,3)--(32,3)--cycle); fill((34,2)--(35,2)--(35,3)--(34,3)--cycle); fill((30,4)--(31,4)--(31,5)--(30,5)--cycle); fill((32,4)--(33,4)--(33,5)--(32,5)--cycle); fill((34,4)--(35,4)--(35,5)--(34,5)--cycle); fill((31,5)--(32,5)--(32,6)--(31,6)--cycle); fill((33,5)--(34,5)--(34,6)--(33,6)--cycle); fill((31,1)--(32,1)--(32,2)--(31,2)--cycle); fill((33,1)--(34,1)--(34,2)--(33,2)--cycle); fill((32,0)--(33,0)--(33,1)--(32,1)--cycle); fill((32,6)--(33,6)--(33,7)--(32,7)--cycle);  draw((32,0)--(33,0)--(33,7)--(32,7)--cycle); draw((29,3)--(36,3)--(36,4)--(29,4)--cycle); draw((30,2)--(35,2)--(35,5)--(30,5)--cycle); draw((31,1)--(31,6)--(34,6)--(34,1)--cycle); [/asy]\" width=\"95\" height=\"95\" />\n</p><p>Shade in the figure so that the outermost squares are shaded and the rest of the grid follows a checkerboard pattern.  Notice that figure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57efb5c0b218135087ac110b8467426b528d7856.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"19\" /> shaded unit squares and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/0/6006bba637eef443381fbaff768fa62d9ff41e62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"17\" /> unshaded unit squares for a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/a/f7ab9bb61eb450bdcdb789abf568bb894cff5600.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+1)^2 + n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"106\" height=\"21\" /> squares.  Therefore, figure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/8/5f8dd4f5f9cad0e31d58b9d92e3b341fee14e0e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$101^2+100^2=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 20201}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"209\" height=\"30\" /> unit squares.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that figure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> consists of a central unit square and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> rings, where for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/c/f5c86874f9ae7f3e00104cc067b560ccbe8ddc0b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\leq k \\leq n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"78\" height=\"15\" />, the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/3/d13e8581eba3a8d29c711befadc131752a022626.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\mathrm{th}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> ring contains <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/f/8dfc0a791aaa72566aa16f59251abceb117de6ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4k$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" /> unit squares.  So figure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> contains <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/6/7769ff39c5282c47f15ed7d14c65be08be130447.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + 4 + 8 + 12 + ... + 400 = 1 + 4(1+2+...+100)=1+4\\left(\\frac{100\\cdot101}{2}\\right) = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C)}\\ 20201}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"688\" height=\"45\" /> unit squares.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can see that each figure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> has a central box and 4 columns of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> boxes on each side of each square. Therefore, at figure 100, there is a central box with 100 boxes on the top, right, left, and bottom. Knowing that each quarter of each figure has a pyramid structure, we know that for each quarter there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/f/e7f66992f43b38f411322643a5ad629ad6cd1900.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{n=1}^{100} n = 5050$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"99\" height=\"50\" /> squares. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/f/63fbb8c2ed5f8d67f1ffe593eaade433e69ae001.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 \\cdot 5050 = 20200$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"127\" height=\"13\" />. Adding in the original center box we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/3/fe3dbab1f3804209d233dce1efa9655ebd936546.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20200 + 1 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }20201}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"193\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" /> be the number of squares in figure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.  We can easily see that \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/c/5dc9a74825f56cc67818fcb8da61611aa619e414.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_0=4\\cdot 0+1\\]\" width=\"102\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/3/42333188c291802f779e9ada597c0c1ecd0f7612.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_1=4\\cdot 1+1\\]\" width=\"102\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/e/17e5662f437a0dd809f4c98d4af2fda33df460a4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_2=4\\cdot 3+1\\]\" width=\"102\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/9/40989a0be2f62369eb9f61dc773f48cee14772fa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_3=4\\cdot 6+1.\\]\" width=\"107\" height=\"15\" />\nSee that we multiply the number we are on to the next consecutive number. \nNote that in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" />, the number multiplied by the 4 is the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />th triangular number.  Hence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/5/3c57dd02b9aeefeca9e31b6711962d5ad4ba477f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{100}=4\\cdot \\frac{100\\cdot 101}{2}+1=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }20201}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"296\" height=\"37\" />.\n~ edited by mathlover66\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/1/fc19ca9685e2dcc4316b6dda7d448dad7254d724.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"17\" height=\"16\" /> denote the number of unit cubes in a figure. We have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/8/a68548667e85fb7a1ceb529aeb3975604cbd3e7e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f_0=1\\]\" width=\"48\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/9/df9da1dce71b4af5aa60ab84bd8ae2b8ed11a38d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f_1=5\\]\" width=\"48\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/e/77e6d1a8da60db4cac72066a0c7a0c30ddc9b778.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f_2=13\\]\" width=\"57\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/a/a7ad38505436381fe9a4cdf07181bf8441742b3a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f_3=25\\]\" width=\"57\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/d/8cd035244016a30d66fc6fb7b3a369de529b337a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f_4=41\\]\" width=\"57\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/7/a77a80061f2acc5bff28985d471dadc862f24425.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[...\\]\" width=\"12\" height=\"2\" />\n</p><p>Computing the difference between the number of cubes in each figure yields\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/2/232c75d3df10e5c1d636b116ab65f6f20e914ece.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[4,8,12,16,...\\]\" width=\"100\" height=\"16\" />\nIt is easy to notice that this is an arithmetic sequence, with the first term being <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and the difference being <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Let this sequence be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f1a2b6b7c193f9ba2b2134a59f1da5addcfbc98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>From <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/9/dc996570a1dbe87c088b9d65db29e6f0f54bfc62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"16\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/2/762528c54833c81f3398803e5b0fb51b07eca52a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_{100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" />, the sequence will have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> terms. Using the arithmetic sum formula yields\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/b/81b7a889e6da0f89d4ecdfb661bca09a9153c685.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[S_{100}=\\frac{100[2\\cdot 4+(100-1)4]}{2}\\]\" width=\"230\" height=\"38\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/6/7e61189e062c7042382a690103c492e3ab31aa7c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[=50(2\\cdot 4+99\\cdot 4)\\]\" width=\"143\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/4/354d5197b9067499660dc344446baf87bc8fe7bc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[=50(101\\cdot 4)\\]\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/b/06be38292ce7677b10f31101d9faba281ec327a6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[=200\\cdot 101\\]\" width=\"84\" height=\"13\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/8/328f29ab41ca9bd4a339aa2c91ae975d20f84dd5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[=20200\\]\" width=\"63\" height=\"12\" />\n</p><p>So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/f/abf2369097d90ac08326602a8f58b83275985c27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_{100}=1+20200=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }20201}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"248\" height=\"30\" /> unit cubes.\n</p><p>~ljlbox\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28Newton.27s_Forward_Differences.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(Newton's_Forward_Differences)\">Solution 6 (Newton's Forward Differences)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (Newton&#39;s Forward Differences)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We know that 1 and 5 differ by 4, 5 and 13 differ by 8, and 13 and 25 differ by 12. Hence the differences are 4, 8, and 12, resp. And the differences of the differences area all 4. So by Newton's Forward Difference Formula, we get the 100th figure is(because Figure 0 exists) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/5/77525b050602ae6f68d36d58ace0b7170f2be51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dbinom{101-1}{0}+4\\dbinom{101-1}{1}+4\\dbinom{101-1}{2}=20201$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"391\" height=\"53\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/9/169b8d69bd74a91e72ec43123070d770633a340a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(C)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" />\n-vsamc\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_7_.28Newton.E2.80.99s_little_formula.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7_(Newton’s_little_formula)\">Solution 7 (Newton’s little formula)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7 (Newton’s little formula)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Newton’s little formula states that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/2/e32907078ccc6b697738a132f23dda5e3b749990.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_n = A \\binom{n-1}{0} + B \\binom{n-1}{1} + C \\binom{n-1}{2} + \\cdots + K \\binom{n-1}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"496\" height=\"55\" /> if first term is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/b/1ab39d761413804680d26d972381f028001562f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/6/f76be31925425c9b26a3075c2cd1c4a2cf404813.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A =$\" width=\"32\" height=\"13\" /> first difference, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/e/00eeacda65171fdf73e0bd6e5ba1dacc9dc991a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B =$\" width=\"33\" height=\"12\" /> second difference, and so on. Hence we apply the formula (because we start at term 0, term 100 is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/9/9e935bcfad39e27904d6834ee8171dfbc47fa668.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{101}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"32\" height=\"13\" />): <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/6/106c1cc8938baeb721527bd4f23479d10fe17577.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{101} = 5 \\binom{100}{0} + 8 \\binom{100}{1} + 4 \\binom{100}{2} = \\boxed{20201} = \\textbf{(C)}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"433\" height=\"55\" />\n~Peelybonehead\n~clarification by LeonidasTheConquerer\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_8_.28geometrical_intuition.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_8_(geometrical_intuition)\">Solution 8 (geometrical intuition)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 8 (geometrical intuition)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>By taking figure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, putting the centers of each square on a grid, and rotating the figure 45 degrees, we get the following shape (figure 3 is shown for reference):\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/3/dc3eafe8923d3810180212e7f65a638e8236b465.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import olympiad;  int fig = 3;  for (int i = 0; i &lt;= 2*fig; ++i) {   for (int j = 0; j &lt;= 2*fig; ++j) {     pair p = (i, j);     //do we need this point?     if ((i+j)%2 == 1) {       continue;     }     //draw squares     draw((p+N)--(p+E)--(p+S)--(p+W)--cycle);     //draw lattices     if (i &lt; 2*fig-1) {       draw(p--p+2*E, red);     }     if (j &lt; 2*fig-1) {       draw(p--p+2*N, red);     }     //mark center points     if (i % 2 == 0) {       dot(p, green);     } else {       dot(p, blue);     }   } } [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"252\" />\nThere are two lattices of points (shown in red): one of side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> (blue) and one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> (green). The rest follows like in solution 1.\n</p><p>-<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User:Integralarefun\" title=\"User:Integralarefun\">Integralarefun</a> (<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User_talk:Integralarefun\" title=\"User talk:Integralarefun\">talk</a>) 18:21, 29 July 2023 (EDT)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_9\">Solution 9</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 9\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If you do not want to base off the previous number in the sequence, you can come up with a formula based on which figure you are looking for:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/8/cf86950aa9e2dd1b9ce45532ec5600effa050348.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+x(x-2+x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"132\" height=\"20\" />\n</p>\n<ul><li>However, this formula says that in figure 1, there is 1 square and so forth. Therefore, if we are looking to find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/3/40396192d77313fb03786134c12eecfed6b5fd3f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(100)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" />, we will have to find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/d/09d0e88c58f6114e13d3871afc23583465995931.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(101)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"54\" height=\"20\" />.</li></ul>\n<p>This is the formula because to get from 1 to 5, we have +4. To get 1 to 13, it is +12. To get 1 to 25, it is +24. Assuming that 5 squares is figure 2 rather than in figure one (sorry, it just made better sense in my brain), then in figure two, it is 2*2 to get +4 on top of one. In figure “3”, to get +12, you have 3*4. In figure 4, to get +24, you have 4*6. Now, you can notice that in figure two, it is 2* (the distance to 2- which is 0- added onto 2) which is +2*2. In figure 3, to get +12, it is 3 (the figure number) * (the distance from 3 to 2- which is 1, added back onto the figure number). In figure 4, to get +24, you can get 4*6 (the figure number * (the distance from 4 to two- which is 2- added back onto the figure number, 6). \n</p><p>So to get figure 100, we have to do f(101) because I said that figure 1 was 1 rather than figure 0. So 1+ 101(101-2+101) which is 20201.\n</p><p>-Smartgrowth\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Power_Solve\">Video Solution by Power Solve</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Power Solve\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/p3bwMuK1RUM?si=1ZOdEq2kRS8DnOVs&amp;t=263\">https://youtu.be/p3bwMuK1RUM?si=1ZOdEq2kRS8DnOVs&amp;t=263</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/y7WrXbKEh18?si=T5jENfI9Wa9UJLuo\">https://youtu.be/y7WrXbKEh18?si=T5jENfI9Wa9UJLuo</a>\n</p><p>~Thesmartgreekmathdude\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=14\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12\" title=\"2000 AMC 12\">2000 AMC 12</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Answer_Key\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 7\">Problem 7</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 9\">Problem 9</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2000_AMC_12_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2000 AMC 12 Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.